    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d281">

                <pb facs="1232" n="1170" sameAs="frus:frus1945v01/pg1170"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.CC/6–545: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Ambassador in the
                        Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Harriman</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-06-05T20:00:00-04:00">June 5, 1945—8
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1228. For the Ambassador and Mr. Hopkins. The following message is from the
                    Secretary and is sent to you with the approval of the President: <pb facs="1233"
                        n="1171" xml:id="pg1171"/>
                    <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“Monday evening in our cable to you<anchor n="26" xml:id="d281-f1"/> we
                            expressed the thought that we could clarify further the original
                            proposed statement on the voting procedure in the Security Council.
                            After several attempts we find that we are unable to do so. It is now
                            obvious that such clarification would not meet both the wishes of the
                            Soviet delegation and ourselves. Therefore, we must stand upon the text
                            of our original statement and explanations given in our previous
                            telegrams of last Saturday and Sunday.<anchor n="27" xml:id="d281-f2"/>
                        </p>
                        <p>“If you have not concluded your discussion with the Marshal about the
                            veto I suggest that you urge that the President feels it is essential
                            that the Marshal agree to our interpretation in order to secure
                            ratification by the Senate.”<anchor n="28" xml:id="d281-f3"/>
                        </p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d281-f1" n="26" xml:id="d281-f1-content">
                <p>Telegram 1219, June 5, 4 a.m., to Moscow, Informed Ambassador Harriman and Mr.
                    Hopkins that a second conversation had been held with Soviet officials the
                    evening of June 4 regarding the proposed interpretative statement on voting
                    procedure in the Security Council and that it seemed that the Soviet officials
                    had seriously misunderstood the original text (500.CC/6–545).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d281-f2" n="27" xml:id="d281-f2-content">
                <p>Telegrams 1203, June 2, 6 p.m., and 1212, June 3, 2 a.m., to Moscow, and
                    telegrams 3 and 4, June 3, from San Francisco, <ref target="#pg1117">pp.
                        1117</ref>, <ref target="#pg1119">1119</ref>, <ref target="#pg1131"
                        >1131</ref>, and <ref target="#pg1136">1136</ref>, respectively.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d281-f3" n="28" xml:id="d281-f3-content">
                <p>In telegram 1930, June 6, 8 p.m., from Moscow, Mr. Hopkins sent the following
                    message to Acting Secretary Grew for President Truman and Mr. Stettinius:
                    “Stalin agrees to accept the United States position regarding voting procedure
                    in the Council. Details follow.” (500.CC/6–645)</p>
                <p>For additional information on the June 6th meeting, see Robert E. Sherwood, <hi
                        rendition="#italic"> Roosevelt and Hopkins</hi>, pp. 910–912.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
